1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Arjun (Pre-Sales): Hi Meera, good afternoon. Can you hear me clearly?

2
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:28,000
Meera (Client): Yes, loud and clear. Thanks for joining. I know calendars are crazy these days.

3
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:42,000
Arjun: Absolutely. I always say, the hardest part of any deal is getting everyone in the same virtual room. How’s your week going so far?

4
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:57,000
Meera: Busy but good. We’re preparing for a board update next week, so conversations like this help me sharpen what I want to present.

5
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:13,000
Arjun: Perfect. Then let’s make this session useful. My goal is not to pitch blindly, but to understand your product, your challenges, and what you expect from a partner. Then we can tailor a proposal. Does that sound fair?

6
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:22,000
Meera: That’s exactly what we need. No generic slides, please. We’ve seen too many of those.

7
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:44,000
Arjun: (laughs) Don’t worry. Let’s start with your product. Could you give me a high-level overview—what you do, who your customers are, and where you are in the product journey?

8
00:01:44,500 --> 00:02:10,000
Meera: Sure. We’re building a B2B SaaS platform focused on logistics optimization. Route planning, live shipment tracking, and predictive delays. We launched an MVP six months ago, onboarded three mid-sized logistics providers, and now we’re aiming for enterprise accounts.

9
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:32,000
Arjun: Great. And when you say scaling, is it infrastructure scaling, functional scaling, or both?

10
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:54,000
Meera: Both. Today, we support ~1,000 shipments/day. Enterprise prospects will expect 100K daily with near real-time dashboards. On top of that, we need better integrations, analytics, and compliance.

11
00:02:54,500 --> 00:03:12,000
Arjun: Understood. Could you walk me through your current architecture? Which cloud, databases, monitoring tools?

12
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:40,000
Meera: AWS—EC2 for compute, RDS for relational data, S3 for storage. We don’t use Kubernetes yet. Monitoring is basic CloudWatch. No central logging. Manual deployments with scripts. DevOps maturity is low, to be honest.

13
00:03:40,500 --> 00:04:08,000
Arjun: That’s common in MVP stages. What programming stack do you use on the application side?

14
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:29,000
Meera: Backend is Node.js with Express. Frontend is React. Data science models in Python, hosted as separate services. Our engineers are solid on coding features, less on infra best practices.

15
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:55,000
Arjun: Got it. So you’d value both execution and consultative guidance. Now—compliance. Are you targeting SOC 2, ISO, GDPR?

16
00:04:55,500 --> 00:05:12,000
Meera: SOC 2, definitely. Clients won’t sign without it. GDPR is secondary but relevant. SOC 2 Type I this year, Type II next.

17
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:40,000
Arjun: Perfect. We’ve guided startups through that—auditor prep, control mapping, automation tools. Now, let’s discuss competitors. Who comes up in client conversations?

18
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:55,000
Meera: Project44, FourKites. They dominate enterprise. Our positioning is AI-driven optimization for mid-market clients—cheaper, faster deployment.

19
00:05:55,500 --> 00:06:18,000
Arjun: Smart angle. To beat them, you’ll need speed of insights, reliability, and smooth integrations. Speaking of AI—how advanced are your models today?

20
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:40,000
Meera: Honestly, still early. One data scientist, using public libraries. Models for ETA estimation and route suggestions. Nothing enterprise-grade yet.

21
00:06:40,500 --> 00:07:05,000
Arjun: That’s fine. We can help with ML Ops, data pipelines, even co-developing optimization engines. Let’s talk integrations: what systems do prospects expect you to plug into?

22
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:28,000
Meera: ERP systems—SAP, Oracle NetSuite. Also carrier APIs. We handle 3–4 now, but scaling to dozens is tricky with our current setup.

23
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:53,000
Arjun: That’s where API gateways, queues, and observability matter. We’ve built that for logistics firms before. I’ll share case studies. Now, how’s your security posture? Do you have IAM policies, encryption, audits?

24
00:07:53,500 --> 00:08:17,000
Meera: Basic IAM. Encryption at rest on RDS. No regular audits. Security hasn’t been a primary focus—though prospects are starting to ask about it.

25
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:45,000
Arjun: That’s important. We’ll need to embed security early. What about your team? How many engineers, and what’s their split?

26
00:08:45,500 --> 00:09:10,000
Meera: Around 12 engineers. Eight backend/frontend, two mobile, one data scientist, one QA. We don’t have a dedicated DevOps or infra engineer.

27
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:36,000
Arjun: Okay. So part of our role could be augmenting with DevOps, cloud architects, and compliance consultants, while your engineers focus on features. Makes sense?

28
00:09:36,500 --> 00:10:00,000
Meera: Yes, that’s exactly the gap we want to fill.

29
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:26,000
Arjun: Let’s talk timelines. When do you expect to close your first enterprise deal?

30
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:44,000
Meera: Ideally within 6 months. We’re in late-stage discussions with two prospects. Their biggest concerns are compliance readiness and scalability.

31
00:10:44,500 --> 00:11:12,000
Arjun: Understood. Then our work should align with that timeline. Phase 1 could be architecture redesign + compliance roadmap, Phase 2 scaling to 50K shipments, Phase 3 AI enhancements.

32
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:34,000
Meera: That phased approach makes sense. It’ll also be easier to justify to the board.

33
00:11:34,500 --> 00:12:02,000
Arjun: Speaking of the board—what’s their appetite for budget? Are you looking for a lean, minimal setup, or are you open to investing for long-term efficiency?

34
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:25,000
Meera: Budget is always tight, but we’re willing to invest where ROI is clear. For example, auto-scaling infra is easier to justify than speculative AI research.

35
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:50,000
Arjun: Makes sense. We’ll map costs for each phase, with clear ROI points—like reduced downtime, faster onboarding, or new client wins. That usually resonates with boards.

36
00:12:50,500 --> 00:13:16,000
Meera: That would be very helpful.

37
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:40,000
Arjun: Let’s talk competitors in detail. What do clients like about Project44, and where do you think they fall short?

38
00:13:40,500 --> 00:14:10,000
Meera: Clients say Project44 is stable and reliable, but it’s expensive, slow to customize, and implementation takes months. We want to be quicker, more flexible.

39
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:35,000
Arjun: Good. So your edge is agility and cost. Our proposal can strengthen that by ensuring infra and integrations don’t become bottlenecks.

40
00:14:35,500 --> 00:15:02,000
Meera: Exactly.

41
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:30,000
Arjun: Now, what about your go-to-market plan? Do you have a dedicated sales team?

42
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:52,000
Meera: Small team—2 salespeople plus me handling enterprise pitches. We rely heavily on referrals and networks right now.

43
00:15:52,500 --> 00:16:20,000
Arjun: Okay, so infra maturity will also become a sales lever. Sales teams can say: “We’re SOC 2 ready, we integrate with SAP in weeks, and we scale seamlessly.”

44
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:40,000
Meera: Yes, that’s the vision.

45
00:16:40,500 --> 00:17:05,000
Arjun: Let me ask—what’s your procurement process? Do you need RFPs, or can we engage directly?

46
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:24,000
Meera: For now, we can engage directly. If we move to multi-year contracts, the board will want RFPs. But discovery and pilot work can start without that.

47
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:52,000
Arjun: That speeds things up. What about geography? Are you targeting India only, or global?

48
00:17:52,500 --> 00:18:12,000
Meera: India + Southeast Asia initially. Enterprise prospects in Singapore are very interested.

49
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:34,000
Arjun: Then compliance frameworks like GDPR, PDPA (Singapore) will matter. We’ll align proposals accordingly.

50
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:55,000
Meera: Good point.

51
00:18:55,500 --> 00:19:20,000
Arjun: To recap—today you have an MVP, small clients, AWS infra, early ML, no DevOps, no compliance readiness. In 12 months, you want enterprise-grade infra, SOC 2, 100K shipments/day, and differentiated AI features.

52
00:19:20,500 --> 00:19:35,000
Meera: That’s correct.

53
00:19:35,500 --> 00:20:00,000
Arjun: Here’s what we’ll do: prepare a tailored proposal with (1) Architecture redesign + scaling plan, (2) Compliance roadmap, (3) AI/ML enhancement, (4) Cost modeling by phases.

54
00:20:00,500 --> 00:20:15,000
Meera: Perfect.

55
00:20:15,500 --> 00:20:42,000
Arjun: I’ll also suggest a short technical workshop between our architects and your engineers next week. That way, we don’t over-engineer or under-engineer—we align on real workloads.

56
00:20:42,500 --> 00:21:02,000
Meera: That’s a good idea.

57
00:21:02,500 --> 00:21:30,000
Arjun: Great. For next steps—could you share architecture diagrams, DB sizes, current costs, and pipeline details? That’ll make our proposal sharper.

58
00:21:30,500 --> 00:21:50,000
Meera: Yes, I’ll coordinate with our CTO. Might take a few days.

59
00:21:50,500 --> 00:22:15,000
Arjun: No rush. Once we have it, we’ll finalize and share a draft proposal in 4–5 working days. Then we can review together.

60
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:35,000
Meera: Sounds good.

61
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:55,000
Arjun: Perfect. Before we wrap—do you have any concerns or red flags about working with partners like us?

62
00:22:55,500 --> 00:23:22,000
Meera: Honestly, past partners have overpromised and underdelivered. I just want realism—what can be done, what can’t. No sugarcoating.

63
00:23:22,500 --> 00:23:42,000
Arjun: Completely fair. We’ll be transparent. If something takes 6 months, we’ll say it. Better than disappointing later.

64
00:23:42,500 --> 00:24:08,000
Meera: Good to hear.

65
00:24:08,500 --> 00:24:35,000
Arjun: Alright then. Thanks, Meera. This was a very productive conversation. Looking forward to collaborating.

66
00:24:35,500 --> 00:25:00,000
Meera: Thank you, Arjun. Likewise.

67
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:20,000
[Small talk as both smile and exchange pleasantries before leaving the call.]
